"An analytic approach to cardinalities of sumsets (https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04075)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Title: An analytic approach to cardinalities of sumsets\nTitle: An analytic approach to cardinalities of sumsets\nAuthors: Dávid Matolcsi, Imre Ruzsa, George Shakan, Dmitrii Zhelezov\nDate: Mon Mar  9 12:36:46 2020\n\nLet $d$ be a positive integer and $U \\subset \\mathbb{Z}^d$ finite. We study $$\\beta(U) : = \\inf_{\\substack{A , B \\neq \\emptyset \\\\ \\text{finite}}} \\frac{|A+B+U|}{|A|^{1/2}{|B|^{1/2}}},$$ and other related quantities. We employ tensorization, which is not available for the doubling constant, $|U+U|/|U|$. For instance, we show $$\\beta(U) = |U|,$$ whenever $U$ is a subset of $\\{0,1\\}^d$. Our methods parallel those used for the Pr\\'ekopa-Leindler inequality, an integral variant of the Brunn-Minkowski inequality.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35550)\nciteturn5academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Gao, Matthew H. ... Date: Mon Sep 28 16:25:02 2026\n\nWe prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid. ... The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids.\nTitle: Nonnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids\nAuthors: Alice L. L. Gao, Matthew H. Y. Xie\nDate: Mon Sep 28 16:25:02 2026\n\nWe prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid. We first show that every simple, cosimple, connected split matroid $M$ has an element $e$ for which both the deletion $M\\setminus e$ and the contraction $M/e$ are connected. More generally, let $M$ be any connected split matroid of rank $k$ on a ground set $E$ with $|E|\\ge4$. Suppose that $e\\in E$ is such that both $M\\setminus e$ and $M/e$ are connected. For every $v\\in E\\setminus\\{e\\}$, we construct a connected elementary split matroid $N_{e,v}$ of rank $k-1$ on $E\\setminus\\{e,v\\}$ satisfying \\[ g_M(t)=g_{M\\setminus e}(t)+g_{M/e}(t)+t\\,g_{N_{e,v}}(t). \\] Using a fixed total order on $E$, we prescribe the proper cyclic flats of $N_{e,v}$ and their ranks. The identity follows from the covaluative formula of Ferroni and Schröter together with recurrences for its correction polynomials, derived from Ferroni's enumeration of admissible Delannoy paths for Schubert matroids. Since all three matroids on the right have fewer elements, the identity supplies the induction step in the proof of nonnegativity.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNUMERICAL IRREDUCIBILITY CRITERIA FOR HANDLEBODY LINKS (https://usiena-air.unisi.it/bitstream/11365/1115614/4/Bellettini%20numerical%20irreducibility%20pre%20print.pdf)\nciteturn5search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Note that a cutting sphere 𝒮 of a reducible handlebody link HL factorizes it into two handlebody links HL₁, HL₂, where HLᵢ := HL ∩ Bᵢ, and Bᵢ, i = 1, 2, are the closures of components of the complement S³ \\ 𝒮 (Fig. ... In fact, it is not known whether we have an affirmative answer to Conjecture 1.1 or Conjecture 1.2¹. ... ** *Every reducible handlebody link admits a minimal diagram whose underlying plane graph is 1-edge-connected.\n1.1); the factorization is denoted by\n\nHL = (HL₁, h₁) -- (HL₂, h₂),  (1.1)\n\nand we call HLᵢ, i = 1, 2, a factor of the factorization, where h₁, h₂ are components of HL₁, HL₂ containing D, respectively.\n\nHandlebody links are often studied and visualized via diagrams of their spines [3]; it is, however, not an easy task to detect the irreducibility of a handlebody link from its diagram. The complexity lies in the IH-move [3]. In fact, it is not known whether we have an affirmative answer to Conjecture 1.1 or Conjecture 1.2¹.\n\n**Conjecture 1.1.** *Every reducible handlebody link admits a minimal diagram whose underlying plane graph is 1-edge-connected.*\n\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classification. 57M25, 57M27.  \nKey words and phrases. reducibility, handlebody links, knot sum.  \n¹Conj. 1.1 implies Conj. 1.2 in some special cases [14, Theorem 2] and [1, Theorem 6.1].\n\nFigure 1.1. A reducible handlebody link and its factors\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 1.1 shows a reducible handlebody link HL on the left with a cutting sphere 𝒮 and disk D inside the shaded region B₁; the middle/right illustration shows the factorization into HL₁ and HL₂, with labels B₁, HL, HL₁, HL₂, h₁, and h₂.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimal-diagram conjecture for reducible handlebody links | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/345521/minimal-diagram-conjecture-for-reducible-handlebody-links)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Minimal-diagram conjecture for reducible handlebody links ... Every reducible handlebody link admits a minimal diagram whose underlying plane graph is $1$1-edge-connected.\n\n# Minimal-diagram conjecture for reducible handlebody links\n\nAbout 6 years old · traced to 2020\n\nA handlebody link $\\operatorname{HL}$HL is a union of finitely many handlebodies of positive genus embedded in the $3$3-sphere $\\mathbb S^3$S 3. It is reducible if there is a cutting $2$2-sphere intersecting $\\operatorname{HL}$HL transversally in an incompressible disk; otherwise it is irreducible. A diagram is minimal if it realizes the crossing number, and its underlying plane graph is called $1$1-edge-connected when it remains connected after deleting any one edge. Minimal-diagram conjecture. Every reducible handlebody link admits a minimal diagram whose underlying plane graph is $1$1-edge-connected. The conjecture concerns whether irreducibility can be detected from a suitably chosen minimal spine diagram; the source states that an affirmative answer is not known.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nGiovanni Bellettini, Maurizio Paolini and Yi-Sheng Wang, “Numerical irreducibility criteria for handlebody links”, arXiv:2002.05974 (2020).\n\ngraph-theoryhandlebody-linksknot-theory\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvery Nonnegative Integer Is a Sum of a Triangular, a Pentagonal, and a Heptagonal Number (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.26035)\nciteturn5academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Authors: Yichuan Cao, Dakai Guo, Ruichen Qiu, Ruyong Feng, Xiao-Shan Gao\nDate: Wed Jun 24 17:11:12 2026\n\nIn this paper, it is proved that any nonnegative integer can be written in the following form $$ x(x+1)/2 + y(3y+1)/2 + z(5z+1)/2, \\qquad x,y,z \\in \\mathbb{N}. $$ This settles the conjecture recorded as OEIS A287616. All parts of the proof have been formalized in Lean 4, with the exception of two results: one externally cited theorem and one statement verified by symbolic computation.\nTitle: Every Nonnegative Integer Is a Sum of a Triangular, a Pentagonal, and a Heptagonal Number\nAuthors: Yichuan Cao, Dakai Guo, Ruichen Qiu, Ruyong Feng, Xiao-Shan Gao\n